---
/** `/privacy/`: what the site keeps about event guests and how they control it. STATIC. */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {staticContext} from '../../server/page';

export const prerender = true;

const {site, viewer} = await staticContext(Astro.request);
---

<Base site={site} viewer={viewer} isStatic guest title="Privacy" description="What we keep when you RSVP to an event, why, for how long, and how to download, correct or erase it.">
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="g-main"><div class="page-container g-doc">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What we keep</h2>
      <p>
        When you RSVP we keep your name, your email, your answer, any answers to the host's questions, and your ticket and check-in
        status. If you buy a ticket we keep the amount and its status, never your card details. If you tick the updates box we keep
        that choice.
      </p>
      <h2>Updates from the host</h2>
      <p>
        Marketing updates are off unless you tick the box when you RSVP, and they only start once you click "Yes, send me updates"
        in the email we send you. Every email we send you has an unsubscribe link. Unsubscribing stops marketing updates and the
        host's announcements to all of your RSVPs with that address; emails about your RSVP itself (confirmation, reminders,
        a cancellation) still arrive.
      </p>
      <h2>Your link</h2>
      <p>
        The email confirming your RSVP has a private link to manage your RSVP and your data. Anyone with the link can use it, so do
        not forward it. If you lose it, ask for a new one on the event page; the old one stops working.
      </p>
      <h2>Download or erase your data</h2>
      <p>
        From your private link you can download everything we hold about you for that event as a JSON file, or erase it. Erasing
        removes your name, email, answers and the host's invitation to you, and frees your seat for the next person on the waitlist.
        Payment records stay for accounting (amount, currency, status and date), without your name, email or a link back to you.
        We keep a one-way fingerprint (a hash) of your email address and the event, only so that we can erase your RSVP again if
        restoring a backup of our data ever brings it back.
      </p>
      <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
      <p>
        Guest details, and the host's invitations to people who never replied, are removed two years after the event ends. Until then
        you can download, erase or unsubscribe at any time, even if the event was cancelled or taken down.
      </p>
      <h2>Visits</h2>
      <p>
        We count event page visits with a hash of your IP address and browser, salted with a random value created each day and deleted
        after two days, so visits are not followed from one day to the next. We do not store your IP address, and once a day's value
        is deleted nobody, us included, can tell from the hash who visited.
      </p>
      <h2>Who handles your data for us</h2>
      <p>
        The site runs on Cloudflare. Emails are sent through our email provider (Resend, when the host has set it up). Ticket payments
        are handled by the payment platform; we never see card details. If the host connects webhooks, your RSVP (name, email,
        answer and status) is sent to the address the host chose. Event cover images may be loaded from the host's image host,
        which then sees your IP address like any website you visit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div></main>
</Base>
